`for … else` in a view body, on all five platforms that render one. The tour
calls the else "a tidy way to show an empty-state message", and before
passViewForElse only bubbletea emitted it: fyne, gtk4, android and html on
--lang none each emitted the loop and nothing at all for the else, so an empty
list rendered an empty region. Silently -- the interpreter walks the IR and
honours Else itself, so `sngl test` passed everywhere.

The rewrite is a conditional on the iterable's emptiness, placed before
reactivity so the `if` reaches it as an ordinary view conditional: on a
mutation-model target (fyne, html) the whole `if` becomes the render slot, so
pushing the button swaps the empty state for the rows and back. Read the
goldens for that -- the else block is inside the slot function on both, not in
the one-time build.

The iterable has to be state for any of this to matter. A const one makes the
conditional's test constant, and the fold after lowering collapses it; it is
the loop that survives to codegen whose else was lost. `rows` is a var for that reason, and `n` likewise.

The emptiness question is asked of the iterable rather than stored, which is
the whole reason this is a second pass and not a wider passForElse. Both
iterable forms a view body can hold are here, because each answers it
differently: a list reports a length, and a sngl:seq range is measured from
its bounds instead, since it has no elements until a host loop counts them --
the step is a constant, so the direction of that comparison is decided at
compile time. A map answers by length as well, and ir.EmptyTest still builds
that test, but the checker refuses a map loop in a view body -- a map yields
its elements in no defined order -- so the third form cannot be written here.
Neither of the two needs anywhere to put a statement, and a view body on
--lang none has nowhere.

deny * `__ran\d` -- a view for-else is a re-evaluated test, never passForElse's flag: a view body on a target with no host language cannot hold the statement that declares one
deny out/none/html/index.html `<span>NOTHING YET</span>` -- the empty state is not baked into the static markup; each of the three slots is an anchor, and which branch fills it is decided in its render function on every change
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:seq"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne(main=true)
        gtk4(gir="builtin", main=true)
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var rows list<string> = []
    var n = 0

    vbox {
        button(text="load", @click {
            rows = ["one", "two"]
            n = 2
        })
        for var row = rows {
            text(value=row)
        } else {
            text(value="NOTHING YET")
        }
        for seq.count(n) {
            text(value="tick")
        } else {
            text(value="NO TICKS")
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
